WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.319
The settlement job writes a two gigabyte file, ledger.csv.

2
00:00:07.319 --> 00:00:14.486
The audit export wants the same file under a second name, ledger-2026.csv, without a copy.

3
00:00:14.486 --> 00:00:17.035
How can one file have two names?

4
00:00:17.233 --> 00:00:22.080
The simple design keeps the size, the owner and the blocks next to the name.

5
00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:25.332
A second name is a second row with the same values.

6
00:00:25.533 --> 00:00:29.770
Append a record through ledger.csv, and only its row grows.

7
00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:32.571
The other row still says two gigabytes.

8
00:00:32.571 --> 00:00:34.151
The two rows disagree.

9
00:00:34.333 --> 00:00:35.994
So Unix splits the row.

10
00:00:35.994 --> 00:00:42.061
The inode is the file itself: size, owner, a count of names, and the list of blocks.

11
00:00:42.061 --> 00:00:44.444
It has a number, 13, and no name.

12
00:00:44.633 --> 00:00:49.739
A directory is a list of entries, each a name and an inode number.

13
00:00:49.739 --> 00:00:54.844
Opening ledger.csv means finding its entry, then reading inode 13.

14
00:00:55.067 --> 00:00:58.540
A hard link is a second entry with the same number.

15
00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:01.672
The inode counts its names, so links is now 2.

16
00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:04.565
Append through either name, and both see it.

17
00:01:04.565 --> 00:01:06.564
There is only one size to change.

18
00:01:06.767 --> 00:01:08.546
rm doesn't delete the file.

19
00:01:08.546 --> 00:01:11.642
It removes one entry and lowers the count to 1.

20
00:01:11.642 --> 00:01:13.421
The other name still works.

21
00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:18.655
Now the audit export, a JVM, opens ledger-2026.csv.

22
00:01:18.655 --> 00:01:23.512
Its file descriptor 4 leads straight to inode 13.

23
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:26.291
Delete the last name, and links is 0.

24
00:01:26.291 --> 00:01:30.561
The kernel still keeps the file, because the JVM has it open.

25
00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:34.602
du adds up the files it can reach by name, and says 0.

26
00:01:34.602 --> 00:01:38.685
df counts the blocks in use, and still says two gigabytes.

27
00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:43.005
When the JVM closes the file, nothing points at the inode.

28
00:01:43.005 --> 00:01:46.401
Only then does the kernel free it, and df drops.

29
00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:48.658
A name only points at an inode.

30
00:01:48.658 --> 00:01:53.504
The kernel frees a file when no name and no open descriptor points at it.
